nodecode-browser-profiles is a rewrite of anpicasso's anpicasso/hermes-plugin-chrome-profiles:

  https://github.com/anpicasso/hermes-plugin-chrome-profiles
  commit 03d24e95e7b14125c5227797a3a6682c3d890c84
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 yuqi (plugin.yaml: "license: MIT", "author: yuqi"; the
  repository carries no LICENSE file of its own)

What was rewritten: the profile model and its resolution — a named profile is a
Chromium-family browser on its own user-data-dir and remote-debugging port, or a
host that is already listening, with the same defaults and the same refusals
(a port outside 1-65535, an unknown browser key, a missing executable, and a
data_dir that is a browser's own default profile directory, whose advice points
at the harness's real-profile setting). The launch is the same: the browser's
binary, --user-data-dir, --remote-debugging-port, the same noise-suppression
flags, --profile-directory when asked, a headless default on a display-less
Unix, a log beside the data dir, a readiness poll bounded by launch_timeout, and
every launched browser terminated on unload. The rest — one active profile, a
listing that reports each profile's port, kind, reachability and errors, a
switch, a disconnect — keeps the original's tool and command names, plus the
DevTools HTTP verbs the original left to the host: version, tabs, open, activate
and close.

What was left out, and why: the original's whole purpose was to set
BROWSER_CDP_URL so the host's other browser tools inherited the switch, and then
to flush the host's live sessions and attach its CDP supervisor on private host
seams (hermes_cli.browser_connect, tools.browser_tool_lifecycle,
tools.browser_tool_cdp). Nodecode has no such variable, no such supervisor and
no CDP consumer to redirect, so those three imports have no equivalent and the
cell speaks CDP itself instead. Page-level driving (clicks, typing, snapshots,
screenshots) needs a CDP WebSocket session, which this cell does not open: only
the HTTP endpoints are used, and the verbs stop at the tab. The original's
listing is answered as text here rather than as JSON, since that is what a tool
answer and evaluation both read well; and the "-profile-directory does not
escape the single-instance lock" probe, which used a private host helper and
psutil, is not restated — Chromium's own launch failure and the log tail in the
refusal carry it.
